----- Demo 1: Troubleshooting Service-Related Issues -----

--1. In the D:\Demofiles\Mod14 folder, run Setup.cmd as Administrator.

--2. In the User Account Control dialog box, click Yes.

--3. Click the Start button, then type Configuration Manager, click SQL Server 2016 Configuration
--Manager.

--4. In the User Account Control dialog box, click Yes.

--5. In Sql Server Configuration Manager, in the left pane, click SQL Server Services.

--6. In right pane, note that the SQL Server (SQL2) service is not running.

--7. Right-click SQL Server (SQL2) and click Start. Note that the service does not start successfully, and
--an error message is returned.

--8. In the SQL Server Configuration Manager dialog, click OK.

--9. To check the Windows system log, click Start, type Event Viewer, and press Enter.

--10. In Event Viewer, in the left pane, expand Windows Logs, and then click System.

--11. Click on the most recent message with a Level of Error and a Source of Service Control Manager.

--12. On the Details tab, note that the error message states that there is a service specific error, and
--provides only the following details:

SQL Server (SQL2)
%%17113

--13. Close the Event Viewer window.

--14. To check the SQL Server error log, start File Explorer, and navigate to C:\Program Files\Microsoft
--SQL Server\MSSQL13.SQL2\MSSQL\Log.

--15. If a Log dialog box appears, click Continue.

--16. Right-click ERRORLOG, click Open with, and then click Notepad. Notice the last three lines of the
--file include the error number displayed in the Windows system log (17113), and a detailed description
--of the problem (scroll to the right to read the full error message). The message indicates that the data
--file for the master database cannot be found.

--17. In File Explorer, navigate to the folder location mentioned in the error message (C:\Program
--Files\Microsoft SQL Server\MSSQL13.SQL2\MSSQL\DATA).

--18. If a DATA dialog box appears, click Continue. Notice that the folder contains the file
--master.AV0001. The demonstration simulates the situation where the master.mdf file has been
--quarantined by an antivirus application, which has renamed it master.AV0001.
--(In a real-world scenario, you would need to recover the file from the quarantine system, and prevent
--the antivirus application from scanning this folder, before attempting to restart the service.)

--19. For the purposes of this demonstration, rename the file master.AV0001 to master.mdf; right-click
--master.AV0001 then click Rename.

--20. Replace the AV0001 file extension with mdf, then press Enter.

--21. In the Rename dialog box, click Yes.

--22. Close File Explorer.

--23. In SQL Server Configuration Manager, in the right pane, right-click SQL Server (SQL2), and then click
--Start. Note that the service starts successfully.

--24. Close SQL Server Configuration Manager and Notepad.



----- Demo 2: DBCC CHECKDB

-- Run DBCC CHECKDB with default options
DBCC CHECKDB('AdventureWorks') WITH ESTIMATEONLY
GO

DBCC CHECKDB('AdventureWorks');
GO

-- Run DBCC CHECKDB without informational messages
DBCC CHECKDB('AdventureWorks') WITH NO_INFOMSGS;
GO

-- Run DBCC CHECKDB against CorruptDB	
DBCC CHECKDB('CorruptDB') WITH NO_INFOMSGS;
GO

-- Try to access the Orders table
SELECT * FROM CorruptDB.dbo.Orders;
GO

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM CorruptDB.dbo.Orders;
GO

-- Access a specific order
SELECT * FROM CorruptDB.dbo.Orders WHERE OrderID = 10400;
GO

